- ### Graphemes and width
+ ### Grafemas y anchura

- A `Char` is not a unit of text a person would recognize. `é` may be one code point or two, and a
- family emoji is several code points joined together, so counting `Char`s answers the wrong
- question. `Writing` gives a text's *grapheme clusters*, which are what a reader would call
- characters, and the boundaries between them:
+ Un `Char` no es una unidad de texto que una persona reconocería. `é` puede ser uno o dos puntos
+ de código, y un emoji de familia son varios puntos de código unidos, así que contar `Char`s
+ responde a la pregunta equivocada. `Writing` da los *grupos de grafemas* de un texto, que son lo
+ que un lector llamaría caracteres, y las fronteras entre ellos:

= characters, and the boundaries between them:
=
- ```scala fuel
+ ```scala fuel

= characters, and the boundaries between them:
=
= ```scala fuel
- run
+ run

= characters, and the boundaries between them:
=
= ```scala fuel
= run
- ##
+ ##

- Out.println(Writing(t"abc").graphemeCount.show)
+ Out.println(Writing(t"abc").graphemeCount.show)

= Out.println(Writing(t"abc").graphemeCount.show)
- ```
+ ```

- Nor is a grapheme always one column wide on a terminal. Under a metric in scope, `metrics` gives
- the width the terminal will actually use. That is one column for ASCII, one for a letter with a
- combining accent, and two for Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters and for most emoji:
+ Tampoco un grafema ocupa siempre una columna de ancho en un terminal. Con una métrica en el
+ ámbito, `metrics` da la anchura que usará realmente el terminal. Es una columna para ASCII, una
+ para una letra con un acento combinante, y dos para los caracteres chinos, japoneses y coreanos
+ y para la mayoría de los emojis:

= combining accent, and two for Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters and for most emoji:
=
- ```scala fuel
+ ```scala fuel

= combining accent, and two for Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters and for most emoji:
=
= ```scala fuel
- run
+ run

= combining accent, and two for Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters and for most emoji:
=
= ```scala fuel
= run
- ##
+ ##

- locally:
+ locally:

-   import textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric
+   import textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric

-   Out.println(Grapheme("a").metrics.show)
+   Out.println(Grapheme("a").metrics.show)

-   Out.println(Grapheme("é").metrics.show)
+   Out.println(Grapheme("é").metrics.show)

-   Out.println(Grapheme("日").metrics.show)
+   Out.println(Grapheme("日").metrics.show)

=   Out.println(Grapheme("日").metrics.show)
- ```
+ ```

- Aligning columns of text in a [terminal](terminal.md) depends on this width.
+ Alinear columnas de texto en un [terminal](terminal.md) depende de esta anchura.

- ### Rendering numbers
+ ### Representar números

- Turning a number into text involves a choice, of how many digits are worth showing, which
- `toString` makes badly. A `Decimalizer` states the choice as a number of significant figures,
- and every `show` of a floating-point number consults the one in scope:
+ Convertir un número en texto implica una elección, la de cuántos dígitos vale la pena mostrar,
+ que `toString` hace mal. Un `Decimalizer` expresa la elección como un número de cifras
+ significativas, y cada `show` de un número de coma flotante consulta el del ámbito:

= and every `show` of a floating-point number consults the one in scope:
=
- ```scala fuel
+ ```scala fuel

= and every `show` of a floating-point number consults the one in scope:
=
= ```scala fuel
- run
+ run

= and every `show` of a floating-point number consults the one in scope:
=
= ```scala fuel
= run
- ##
+ ##

- Out.println(Decimalizer(3).decimalize(-3.14159))
+ Out.println(Decimalizer(3).decimalize(-3.14159))

= Out.println(Decimalizer(3).decimalize(-3.14159))
- ```
+ ```

- The same value therefore renders consistently everywhere in a program, and changing the
- precision is a matter of one given, not a search for format strings. [Numbers](numbers.md)
- covers the numeric types themselves.
+ Por tanto, el mismo valor se representa de forma coherente en todo el programa, y cambiar la
+ precisión es cuestión de un given, no de buscar cadenas de formato. [Números](numbers.md) trata
+ los propios tipos numéricos.

- ### Any textual type
+ ### Cualquier tipo textual

- Every operation above is defined over the `Textual` typeclass, not over `Text` alone. A type
- which declares itself textual gains the same `cut`, `join`, `keep`, `pad` and the rest, without
- redefining any of them. The styled text of a [terminal](terminal.md) is one such type, so styled
- and plain text are manipulated with one vocabulary.
+ Todas las operaciones anteriores se definen sobre la clase de tipos `Textual`, no solo sobre
+ `Text`. Un tipo que se declara textual obtiene los mismos `cut`, `join`, `keep`, `pad` y demás,
+ sin redefinir ninguno. El texto con estilo de un [terminal](terminal.md) es uno de esos tipos,
+ así que el texto con estilo y el texto plano se manipulan con un mismo vocabulario.
